<ContentLayout title="INIU 240W USB-C Cable vs Generic USB-C: Which Actually Charges Faster? | StackGeist" description="Cheap USB-C cables cap at 60W by spec. The INIU 240W has a real e-marker. Here is what the difference actually means for your charging speed." active="gear">
  <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">StackGeist</a> / <a href="/gear">Gear</a> / <a href="/gear/budget-tech">Budget tech</a> / <a href="/gear/budget-tech/compare">Compare</a> / INIU vs generic USB-C</p>
  <p class="page-kicker">Head to head</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">INIU 240W USB-C Cable vs Generic USB-C: Which Actually Charges Faster?</h1>
  <p class="page-lead">Both are cheap, both plug into the same ports, both look identical in product photos. One delivers full-speed USB-PD 3.1 charging. The other quietly throttles your laptop to 60W without telling you. Here is exactly where the $8 difference matters.</p>

  <section class="content-section">
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>INIU 240W USB-C Cable</th><th>Generic USB-C Cable</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody><tr><td>Price</td><td>~$10</td><td>$3-8</td></tr>
<tr><td>Max wattage</td><td>240W (48V/5A)</td><td>60W (20V/3A) by USB-C spec default</td></tr>
<tr><td>E-marker chip</td><td>Yes, verified</td><td>Usually no</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data speed</td><td>USB 2.0 (480 Mb/s)</td><td>USB 2.0 (480 Mb/s)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Display output</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Braided jacket</td><td>Nylon</td><td>Rubber / PVC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warranty</td><td>Lifetime (INIU)</td><td>None to 90 days</td></tr></tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When INIU 240W USB-C Cable wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick INIU 240W USB-C Cable if...</h2>
    <p>You have any device that needs more than 60W to charge fast. That includes: MacBook Pro 14" / 16", most gaming laptops, Framework 13/16, higher-wattage phone fast-charging modes (Xiaomi HyperCharge, OnePlus SuperVOOC).</p><p>You want a cable that will not silently throttle your charger. USB Power Delivery negotiation checks the cable's e-marker chip. No chip means the charger defaults to safe-mode 60W regardless of what the packaging claims. The INIU has a real chip; the certified 240W rating is not a marketing number.</p><p>You want a braided cable that survives being coiled and pulled daily. The nylon jacket on the INIU stands up to years of desk-cable abuse in a way that thin PVC generic cables do not.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When Generic USB-C Cable wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick Generic USB-C Cable if...</h2>
    <p>You only charge a phone or Nintendo Switch at 20-30W and you own a spare cable that lives in a bag. Any generic cable will do this. Paying for 240W headroom you never use is fine but not necessary.</p><p>You need a very short cable (6 inches / 1 foot) for a specific device that stays in one place. Short-run generic cables from a reputable seller (Anker Nano, UGREEN short) at the sub-$5 price point are perfectly fine for low-wattage charging.</p><p>You buy cables in bulk for a household. Generic 5-packs are cheap and if you're only charging phones, the wattage difference genuinely doesn't matter.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">The verdict</p>
    <p>Buy the INIU if you have any modern laptop, or if you own the charger to feed a device that draws over 60W. The $8 upgrade pays for itself the first time your laptop charges in the time the charger claims.</p><p>Buy generic if the only devices in your house draw under 30W and you want cheap backup cables. Just don't complain when the laptop still charges slowly.</p>
  </section>

